3,000-word report on the app

1. The team members who produced the app were Oliver hunt and Oliver Tegg-Quin. This is the GitHub link used for collaboration throughout the app development: https://github.com/oliver25447884/CITS1501-project-1

2. This app focuses on the 6 seasons of the Whadjuk Noongar weather calendar. We used Python to create an app that visualises, via line graphs, average rainfall and temperature for all 6 Noongar seasons and provides context for their spiritual and meteorological meanings. The app was built primarily for educational purposes, for consumers to see a different view of seasonal timing and calendar-making, as Australia follows the 4 European seasons, which don’t accurately reflect Western Australia’s climate. The app serves to create a new lens on how we view WA’s weather, helping users associate daily weather with there indigenous meaning via an intuitive, information-rich, and visually appealing UI.

5. The data was sourced from the Bureau of Meteorology from the Perth metro WA site (station number: 009225) from the year of 2024 January to December, collating the daily rainfall (mm) and temperature (degrees Celsius). The standard python algorithm was used to accurately calculate the mean temperature and rainfall of each month and then calculate a mean for each Noongar season (i.e the 2-month average). Calendar dates were aligned to the Noongar Calendar, not the European version for accurate data modelling; further missing data entries were handled successfully, and the data was converted into a .csv file for accurate reading into Python without loss of data integrity.

6. The application uses the BOM data and analyses the seasonal trends and calculates the mean rainfall and temperature to visualise a complete story about the seasons. Differences between the last season and the next are calculated (i.e. Kambarang gets 19.3mm/month more rain than Birak and is 5.4 degrees cooler). This analysis reveals that WA’s southwest climate categorises better into 2-month intervals, such as distinctive heavy rain in Makuru (June, July) and extreme heat in Bunuru (February, March), than the standard 3-month intervals. To further visualise this to users, the numerical data trend was shown as a line graph of temperature and rainfall averages across the 12 months. The x-axis is labelled with months and Noongar season categories, and the y-axis is labelled with the variable (temperature/rainfall). Intuitive UI design is used, with blue colours for rainfall and red for temperature. 

7. The application has a clean and intuitive UI experience to make climate data, Noongar seasonal analysis, and spiritual meaning accessible and clear for everyday users wanting to know more. The app interface starts with a security page for logging in via a password, and a forgot password option. Further, the main page of the app features an interactive Noongar season-labelled wheel, where the user clicks on a section and is taken to a new page containing a data-rich but clear analysis of the season, with a line graph of the averages in rain (in blue) and temperature (in red) that shows a description of the season's weather conditions and the spiritual meaning of the Noongar people. Further, an image of the characteristic event in that season is showcased for visual clarity. This page setup is used for all 6 noongar seasons. And a Q & A section is easily visible at the bottom of the main page to show common queries from users, such as: What Noongar season is best for exploring the Margaret River region? This is coupled with an interactive overview map of WA’s Southwest Noongar region with black borders. The map allows users to identify which Noongar region they live in for personal context. Overall, the user interface is clean, easy to understand and educationally rich in detail. 

10. This project is deployed in the environment of Python 3.14.7 in the GUI library of Tkinter desktop application for ease of opening and data visualisation. The data visualisation library Matplotlib was used for the line graph creation, as it produces visually appealing and accurate graphs. Further, pandas was used for data analysis when loading the .csv files from BOM; this ensures data integrity and accuracy are maintained. Core assets such as BOM weather data, images and the map diagram were organised within the project directory using file paths. This efficient organisation allows the application to open fully without data loss and without requiring internet access.

